Rally Entry, a new API should be created in rallymaster_server (/compete) to support this.  In the app the user will have two options "Find my event", this option will grab the current GPS location and the server will search for any Rallies/Routes on todays date with a mile of the location.  There should also be a quick link which can be manually entered or scanned from a QR code, this quick link is just a base36 (0-9, a-z) representation of the rally id.  Once the rally is located it will obtain the list of "ACTIVE" routes and allow the competitor to choose which route.  In addition the registration should capture the driver first name/last name and navigator first name/last name as well as car number.  The car number will typically be assigned by the rallymaster but a check should be put in place to ensure unique car numbers

Team Model, there will be once device tied to the team for purposes of official scoring.  During the registration process a backup device can be registered and associated with that team / car number.  The backup device will run and capture scoring information but that scoring information will not be sent to the server.  In the event of a discrepancy or a missed checkpoint on the primary device, the rally master can elect to use the backup score and for now manually enter it as an override in the scoring system on the server.

Checkpoint detection, this is the challenge part because it will need accurate timing for when the competitor crosses the checkpoint including in areas of low GPS accuracy or potentially periods of GPS signal loss.  As for the process, a GPS radius will be establish, when the car enters that radius the GPS location should be supplemented by a Kalman filter and based on points captured within the GPS Radius, the point should be used to calculate the time at which an intercept occurs.  That intercept is the point at which the vehicle course is closest to the checkpoint location.  The heading of the vehicle should be within a cone of 60 degreess of the heading of the checkpoint.  This will handle the case of the competitor being lost and going through the checkpoint in the wrong direction.  The interept point should be within 40 meters of the checkpoint location or it shouldn't count.

Timing, the official time will be the phone time.  When the checkpoints are download from the server, there will be an official start time and every checkpoint will have a duration time which will be the official time it should take to get to that point.   The compete will use the official start based on the phone actual time and then apply the duration to calculate the time the competitor should cross the checkpoint.  Early and Late arrivals are score the same 1 point for each second late or early or if rally time is run in 100ths of a minute (i.e. cents) then 1 point for each cent early or late.  The rally master can also set a maximum score per checkpoint.

Checkpoint Rules, PASSAGE_CONTROL I'm going to rename to OFFCOURSE_CONTROL when one of these is encountered the competitor is considered off course and should be scored the maximum for each checkpoint in the segement.  A segement is defined as all checkpoints between RESTARTs.  TIMED_CONTROL is a checkpoint that should be scored, the scoring is based on the perfect time to arrive at the checkpoint based on adding the duration to the start time.  TIME_CHECK this is a checkpoint that just records it was crossed it does not count towards scoring.  RESTART a RESTART checkpoint is a checkpoint that will reset mileage and it will also have a time delay which will be used to calculate a new start and form the basis for the checkpoint times in a TIMED_CONTROLS in the new segment.  START is just a location stamp and it is used by the server for finding the rally the competitor is registering for.  The FINISH is a location that when crossed can indicate to the RallyMaster that the competitor has finished the rally.

Scoring, each checkpoint is score based on the early/late arrivel at the checkpoint compared to the perfect time for arrive.  It is 1 point for each second early or late or if the rallymaster has choosen to run the Rally in 1/100th of a minute (i.e. cents) it is 1 point for each cent early or late.  The overall score is just the sum of each individual checkpoint score.

False Positives, the heading is used to determine if the competitor enters from the wrong direction and should be ignored if they. For scoring purposes only the first time through the checkpoint should be scored. The previously described logic should handle any speed changes inside the gps radius and anything outside can be completely ignored.

Offline behavior, the app will download the list of checkpoints and be able to compute scores without internet connectivity.  After every scored checked point it should attempt to submit the results to the server and if that fails it should periodically retry.

Privacy/Safety: The live location should be sent no more than every 10 seconds, the actual frequency can be discussed later.  The data is only available to the RallyMaster for purposes of monitoring the event.  The SOS will send the location along with an optional message.  The workflow for handling this will be designed in the future.

Competitor UX:  The primary screen will be a split screen.  Half the screen will be a table that will list checkpoints and the score as it is calculated.  The top half will have a UI where the user can see their car number, rally name, and buttons for adding TA (Time Allowance).  A TA can be used then the competitor knows they are running late and it will be used as an adjustment to calculate the score allowing for the known delay.  Their should also be button for publishing an SOS and for withdrawal.  The rallymaster will have the ability to send messages/alerts and those should appear on screen as a popup which needs to be acknowledged.  The app should continue to score even if there are on acknowledged messages.

Authentication: A password generated by the rallymaster will need to be provided to complete the registration process.  It will be the same password for everyone at the event as the password is only used to prevent non competitors (who wouldn't have the password) from registering.
